Let $A$ be the event that the third card is an ace, and let $H$ be the event that the third card is a heart. Then the probability that the third card is an ace or a heart can be represented as $P(A\cup H).$

Given that the ace of hearts is no longer in the deck, $A$ and $H$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(A \cap H) = 0.$

We can find $P(A)$ and $P(H)$ by computing the necessary ratios. When the third card is drawn, there are $50$ cards in the deck. Among the remaining $50$ cards, there are $13-2=11$ hearts and $4-1=3$ aces. Therefore:
$$
\begin{aligned}
P(A) &= \frac{3}{50} \\
P(H) &= \frac{11}{50}
\end{aligned}
$$

Finally, by the addition law, we have
$$
\begin{array}{rl}\displaystyle P ( A \cup  H ) & \displaystyle  = P ( A ) + P ( H ) - P ( A \cap  H ) \\ \displaystyle  & \displaystyle  = {\displaystyle \frac{3}{50}} + {\displaystyle \frac{11}{50}} - 0 \\ \displaystyle  & \displaystyle  = {\displaystyle \frac{14}{50}} \\ \displaystyle  & \displaystyle  = {\displaystyle \frac{7}{25}} .\end{array}
$$

