Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where

- $L = {\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{\color{blue}1} & {\color{red}0} & {\color{red}0} \\ ∗ & {\color{blue}1} & {\color{red}0} \\ ∗ & ∗ & {\color{blue}1}\end{array} \right]}$ is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and

- $U = {\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\ {\color{red}0} & ∗ & ∗ \\ {\color{red}0} & {\color{red}0} & ∗\end{array} \right]}$ is an *upper triangular matrix*.

For example, suppose we're given the matrix $A = {\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}1 & - 1 & - 2 \\ 0 & 2 & - 3 \\ - 2 & 2 & 7\end{array} \right]}.$ To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as 

$$
\underbrace{
{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}1 & - 1 & - 2 \\ 0 & 2 & - 3 \\ - 2 & 2 & 7\end{array} \right]}
}_{A}
=
\underbrace{
{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{\color{blue}1} & {\color{red}0} & {\color{red}0} \\ ∗ & {\color{blue}1} & {\color{red}0} \\ ∗ & ∗ & {\color{blue}1}\end{array} \right]}
}_{L}
\cdot
\underbrace{
{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\ {\color{red}0} & ∗ & ∗ \\ {\color{red}0} & {\color{red}0} & ∗\end{array} \right]}
}_{U}.
$$

As usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:

$$
\begin{aligned}A & =[\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\ -2 & 2 & 7\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \,\overbrace{[\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 2 & 0 & 1\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\cdot A \\  & ∼[\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\ 0 & 0 & 3\end{aligned}]=U\end{aligned}
$$

The reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:

$$
\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\ A & ={[\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 2 & 0 & 1\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\ A & =\underbrace{[\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ -2 & 0 & 1\end{aligned}]}_{L}\underbrace{[\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\ 0 & 0 & 3\end{aligned}]}_{U}\end{aligned}
$$

**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:

- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and

- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed.

There is an algorithm that uses $LU$ factorization to solve systems of equations of the form 

$$
A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b} .
$$

For example, let's solve the following equation:

$$
\underbrace{ {\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 3 \\ - 1 & 2\end{array} \right]}}_{A}

\cdot

\underbrace{
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{array} \right]}
}_{\mathbf x}
=
\underbrace{
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 1 \\ 0\end{array} \right]}
}_{\mathbf b}
$$

Since the LU factorization of $A$ is

$$
A = 
	\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\ - 1 & 1\end{array} \right]}
	}_{L}
	\cdot
	\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 3 \\ 0 & - 1\end{array} \right]}
	}_{U},
$$
our equation $A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}$ can be written as

$$
\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\ - 1 & 1\end{array} \right]}
	}_{L}
	\cdot
	\underbrace{
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 3 \\ 0 & - 1\end{array} \right]}
	}_{U}
\cdot
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{array} \right]}

=

{\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 1 \\ 0\end{array} \right]}
$$

i.e., 

$$
LU\mathbf{x}= \mathbf{b}.
$$

Now, suppose we set $U\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the above, we have

$$
L\mathbf{y}= \mathbf{b}.
$$

In other words, we can reduce the problem to the following system of matrix equations:

$$
\left\{\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\ Ux &=y\end{aligned}\right.
$$

Since $L$ and $U$ are both triangular, each of these equations is easily solvable using back and forward substitution.

- First, we solve $L\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{b}.$ Let's assume that $\mathbf{y}={\left[ \begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\ {y}_{2}\end{array} \right]}.$ Then, we have
$$
{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\ - 1 & 1\end{array} \right]}
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\ {y}_{2}\end{array} \right]}
=
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 1 \\ 0\end{array} \right]}
$$
which gives the system of equations

$$
\left\{\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-1 \\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=0.\end{aligned}\right.
$$

Solving this equation using *forward* substitution, we get the solution
$$
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\ {y}_{2}\end{array} \right]} = {\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 1 \\ - 1\end{array} \right]}.
$$

- Finally, we solve $U\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{y}.$ We have
$$
{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}1 & - 3 \\ 0 & - 1\end{array} \right]}
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{array} \right]}
=
 {\left[ \begin{array}{c}- 1 \\ - 1\end{array} \right]}
$$
which gives the system of equations

$$
\left\{\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=-1 \\ -{x}_{2} &=-1.\end{aligned}\right.
$$

Solving this equation using *back* substitution, we get the solution
$$
{\left[ \begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\ {x}_{2}\end{array} \right]} = {\left[ \begin{array}{c}2 \\ 1\end{array} \right]}.
$$

