\begin{aligned}[\,x\,]_{C} &={P}_{B\to C}\cdot [\,x\,]_{B}.\end{aligned}
$$

In expanded form, the formula looks as follows:
$$
\overbrace{
	\left[
	{\begin{array}{c}| \\ [ \mathbf{x} {]}_{ {\color{red}\mathcal{C}}} \\ |\end{array}}
	\right]
	}^{\large [\,\mathbf{x}\,]_{\color{red}\mathcal{C}}}
	=
	\overbrace{
	\left[
	{\begin{array}{ccc}| &  & | \\ [ {\color{blue}{\mathbf{b}}_{1}} {]}_{ {\color{red}\mathcal{C}}} & \ldots  & [ {\color{blue}{\mathbf{b}}_{n}} {]}_{ {\color{red}\mathcal{C}}} \\ | &  & |\end{array}}
	\right]
	}^{\large P_{\small{\color{blue}\mathcal{B}}\to{\color{red}\mathcal{C}}}}
	\cdot
	\overbrace{
	\left[
	{\begin{array}{c}| \\ [ \mathbf{x} {]}_{ {\color{blue}\mathcal{B}}} \\ |\end{array}}
	\right]
	}^{\large [\,\mathbf{x}\,]_{\color{blue}\mathcal{B}}}
$$

Also, remember that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\color{blue}\mathcal{B}$ to $\color{red}\mathcal{C}$ and from $\color{red}\mathcal{C}$ to $\color{blue}\mathcal{B}$ are the inverses of each other:

$$
P_{\small{\color{red}\mathcal{C}}\to{\color{blue}\mathcal{B}}} = P_{\small{\color{blue}\mathcal{B}}\to{\color{red}\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}
	\quad\textrm{and}\quad
	P_{\small{\color{blue}\mathcal{B}}\to{\color{red}\mathcal{C}}} = P_{\small{\color{red}\mathcal{C}}\to{\color{blue}\mathcal{B}}}^{-1}
$$

Given a matrix $A,$ the diagonalization 

$$
A =
	\underbrace{ {\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{\color{red}|} & {\color{blue}|} \\ {\color{red}{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathbf{1}}} & {\color{blue}{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathbf{2}}} \\ {\color{red}|} & {\color{blue}|}\end{array} \right]}}_{P}
	\underbrace{ {\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{\color{red}{\lambda }_{1}} & 0 \\ 0 & {\color{blue}{\lambda }_{2}}\end{array} \right]}}_{D}
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{\color{red}|} & {\color{blue}|} \\ {\color{red}{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathbf{1}}} & {\color{blue}{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathbf{2}}} \\ {\color{red}|} & {\color{blue}|}\end{array} \right]}^{-1}
$$

is almost unique, but not quite.

When we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ we can put the eigenvalues in any order. So this matrix is unique up to permutations of the order of the eigenvalues. Note that by swapping the eigenvalues in $D,$ we then have to swap the corresponding eigenvectors (columns) in $P{:}$

$$
A = 
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{\color{blue}|} & {\color{red}|} \\ {\color{blue}{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathbf{2}}} & {\color{red}{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathbf{1}}} \\ {\color{blue}|} & {\color{red}|}\end{array} \right]}
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{\color{blue}{\lambda }_{2}} & 0 \\ 0 & {\color{red}{\lambda }_{1}}\end{array} \right]}
	{\left[ \begin{array}{cc}{\color{blue}|} & {\color{red}|} \\ {\color{blue}{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathbf{2}}} & {\color{red}{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathbf{1}}} \\ {\color{blue}|} & {\color{red}|}\end{array} \right]}^{-1}
$$

Also, since any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is an eigenvector as well, the eigenvector basis of $\mathbb{R}^2$ can consist of these multiples instead. So the invertible matrix $P$ is unique up to multiples of its columns.

