In general, there are $6$ possible Jordan canonical forms $J$ for $3\times3$ matrices up to permutations of its Jordan blocks. 

- If $A$ has *three* distinct eigenvalues $\lambda_1, \lambda_2,$  and $\lambda_3,$ then $A$ is diagonalizable, and its corresponding Jordan canonical form $J$ is simply the diagonal form of the matrix. In this case, $J$ is a composition of three $1\times1$ distinct Jordan blocks:
$$
J=
{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{\color{blue}{\lambda }_{1}} & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & {\color{red}{\lambda }_{2}} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & {\color{purple}{\lambda }_{3}}\end{array} \right]}
={ \color{blue}J_1(\lambda_1)}{\:\oplus\:} {\color{red}J_1(\lambda_2)}{\:\oplus\:} {\color{purple}J_1(\lambda_3)}
$$

- If $A$ has *two* distinct eigenvalues $\lambda_1$  and $\lambda_2,$ where $\lambda_1$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$, then there are two possible Jordan canonical forms for $A:$

- If there are two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\lambda_1$, then $J$ is the composition of two $1\times1$ Jordan blocks corresponding to $\lambda_1$ and a $1\times1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\lambda_2{:}$
$$
J=
{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{\color{blue}{\lambda }_{1}} & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & {\color{blue}{\lambda }_{1}} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & {\color{red}{\lambda }_{2}}\end{array} \right]}
= {\color{blue}J_1(\lambda_1)} {\:\oplus\:}  {\color{blue}J_1(\lambda_1)}{\:\oplus\:}  {\color{red}J_1(\lambda_2)}
$$

- If there is only one linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\lambda_1$, then $J$ is the composition of a $2\times2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\lambda_1$ and a $1\times1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\lambda_2{:}$
$$
J=
{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{\color{blue}{\lambda }_{1}} & {\color{blue}1} & 0 \\ {\color{blue}0} & {\color{blue}{\lambda }_{1}} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & {\color{red}{\lambda }_{2}}\end{array} \right]}
= {\color{blue}J_2(\lambda_1)} {\:\oplus\:} {\color{red}J_1(\lambda_2)}
$$

- If $A$ has only *one* eigenvalue $\lambda_1= \lambda_2=\lambda_3= \lambda,$  then there are three possible Jordan canonical forms for $A:$

- If there are three linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\lambda$, then $A$ must be a scalar matrix (meaning that it's already diagonalized), and $J=A$ is a composition of three identical $1\times1$ Jordan blocks:
$$
J=A=\lambda I=
{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{\color{blue}\lambda } & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & {\color{blue}\lambda } & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & {\color{blue}\lambda }\end{array} \right]}
={ \color{blue}J_1(\lambda)}{\:\oplus\:}{ \color{blue}J_1(\lambda)}{\:\oplus\:} { \color{blue}J_1(\lambda)}
$$

- If there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\lambda$, then $J$ is the composition of a $2\times2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\lambda$ and a $1\times1$ Jordan block also corresponding to $\lambda{:}$
$$
J=
{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{\color{blue}\lambda } & {\color{blue}1} & 0 \\ {\color{blue}0} & {\color{blue}\lambda } & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & {\color{red}\lambda }\end{array} \right]}
={ \color{blue}J_2(\lambda)}{\:\oplus\:} { \color{red}J_1(\lambda)}
$$

- Finally,  if there is just one linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to $\lambda$, then $J$ is a single  $3\times3$ Jordan block corresponding to $\lambda{:}$
$$
J=
{\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}{\color{blue}\lambda } & {\color{blue}1} & {\color{blue}0} \\ {\color{blue}0} & {\color{blue}\lambda } & {\color{blue}1} \\ {\color{blue}0} & {\color{blue}0} & {\color{blue}\lambda }\end{array} \right]}
={ \color{blue}J_3(\lambda)}
$$

